\begin_layout Title
Fudenberg Chapter 8 - Equilibrium Refinements: Perfect Bayesian Equilibrium;
 Sequential Equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Perfect Bayesian Equilibrium in Multi-Stage Games of Incomplete Information
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.2.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Basic Signaling Game
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Signaling games are the simplest kind of game in which the issues of updating
 and perfection both arise.
 In these games, there are two players.
 Player 1 is the leader (also called the sender because he sends a signal),
 and player 2 is the follower (or receiver).
 Player 1 has private information about his type 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 and chooses action 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 (we delete the subscript on player 1's type, as this will not lead to confusion.
) Player 2, whose type is cmoon knowledge for simplicity, observe 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

 and chooses 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{2}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The space of mixe actions are 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{A}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{A}_{2}$
\end_inset

 with elements 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Player 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's payoff is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 Before the ame beings, it is common knowledge that player 2 has prior beliefs
 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 about player 1's type.
 A strategy for player 1 prescribes a probability distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}(\cdot|\theta)$
\end_inset

 over actions 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

 for each type 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 A strategy for player 2 prescribes a probability distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{2}(\cdot|a_{1})$
\end_inset

 over actions 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{2}$
\end_inset

 for each action 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Type 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

's payoff to strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}(\cdot|\theta)$
\end_inset

 when player 2 plays 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{2}(\cdot|a_{1})$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2},\theta)={\displaystyle \sum_{a_{1}}}{\displaystyle \sum_{a_{2}}}\sigma_{1}(a_{1}|\theta)\sigma_{2}(a_{2}|a_{1})u_{1}(a_{1},a_{2},\theta)
\]

\end_inset

Player 2's (ex ante) payoff to strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{2}(\cdot|a_{1})$
\end_inset

 when player 1 plays 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}(\cdot|\theta)$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{\theta}}p(\theta)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{a_{1}}}{\displaystyle \sum_{a_{2}}}\sigma_{1}(a_{1}|\theta)\sigma_{2}(a_{2}|a_{1})u_{2}(a_{1},a_{2},\theta)\right)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Player 2, who observes player 1's move before choosing her action, should
 update her beliefs about 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 and base her choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{2}$
\end_inset

 on the posterior distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu(\cdot|a_{1})$
\end_inset

 over 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

.
 How is this posterior formed? In a Bayesian equilibrium, player 1's action
 can depend on his type.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}^{*}(\cdot|\theta)$
\end_inset

 denot this strategy.
 Knowing 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and observing 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

, player 2 can use Bayes' rule to update 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 into 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu(\cdot|a_{1})$
\end_inset

.
 The natural extension of the subgame-perfect equilibrium to the signaling
 game is the perfect Bayesian equilibrium, which requires that player 2
 maximize her payoff conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

, where the conditional payoff to strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{2}(\cdot|a_{1})$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{\theta}}\mu(\theta|a_{1})u_{2}(a_{1},\sigma_{2}(\cdot|a_{1}),\theta)={\displaystyle \sum_{\theta}}{\displaystyle \sum_{a_{2}}}\mu(\theta|a_{1})\sigma_{2}(a_{2}|a_{1})u_{2}(a_{1},a_{2},\theta)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
A 
\emph on
perfect Bayesian equilibrium 
\emph default
(PBE) of a signaling game is a strategy profile 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{*}$
\end_inset

 and posterior beliefs 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu(\cdot|a_{1})$
\end_inset

 such that:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
(\mbox{P}_{1}):\ \forall\ \theta,\ \sigma_{1}^{*}(\cdot|\theta)\in\arg{\displaystyle \max_{\alpha_{1}}}u(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\theta)\\
(\mbox{P}_{2}):\ \forall\ a_{1},\ \sigma_{2}^{*}(\cdot|a_{1})\in\arg{\displaystyle \max_{a_{2}}}{\displaystyle \sum_{\theta}}\mu(\theta|a_{1})u_{2}(a_{1},\alpha_{2},\theta)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(\mbox{B}):\ \mu(\theta|a_{1})=\frac{p(\theta)\sigma_{1}^{*}(a_{1}|\theta)}{{\displaystyle \sum_{\theta^{\prime}\in\Theta}}p(\theta^{\prime})\sigma_{1}^{*}(a_{1}|\theta^{\prime})}
\]

\end_inset

if 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{\theta^{\prime}\in\Theta}}p(\theta^{\prime})\sigma_{1}^{*}(a_{1}|\theta^{\prime})>0$
\end_inset

, and
\begin_inset Formula $\mu(\cdot|a_{1})$
\end_inset

 is any probability distribution on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{\theta^{\prime}\in\Theta}}p(\theta^{\prime})\sigma_{1}^{*}(a_{1}|\theta^{\prime})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{P}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{P}_{2}$
\end_inset

 are the perfeciton conditions.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{P}_{1}$
\end_inset

 says that player 1 takes into account the effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

 on player 2's action; 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{P}_{2}$
\end_inset

 states that player 2 reacts optimally to player 1's action given her posterior
 beliefs about 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{B}$
\end_inset

 corresponds to the applications of Bayes' rule.
 Note that if 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

 is not part of player 1's optimal strategy for some type, observing 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

 is a probability-0 event, and Bayes; rule does not pin down posterior beliefs.
 
\emph on
Any
\emph default
 posterior beliefs 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu(\cdot|a_{1})$
\end_inset

 are then admissible and so any aciton 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{2}$
\end_inset

 can be played that is a best response for some beliefs.
 (This means that the only actions excluded are those which are dominated
 given that 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

 is played.) Indeed, the purpose of the refinements of the perfect Baynesian
 equilibrium concept is to put restrictions on these posterior beliefs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, a PBE is simply a set of strategies and beliefs such that, at any
 stage of the game, strategies are optimal given the beliefs such that,
 at any stage of the game, strategies are optimal given the beliefs, and
 the beliefs are obtianed form equilibrium strategies and observed actions
 using Bayes' rule.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note the link between strategies and beliefs: The beliefs are consistent
 with the strategies, which are optimal given the beliefs.
 Because of this circularity, PBE cannot be determined by backward induction
 when there is incomplete information, even if players move one at a time.
\end_layout

